Z||000009||Philphine||12-18-2001||03:56 PM||||i just turned in the paperwork to have my clearance rechecked for the military. someone decided if it was done more than 10 yrs. ago you may have done something since then. my only fear is it will be so unbelievably boring they'll think i made it up.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>ain't no place like home,<BR>ain't no place like home,<BR>ain't no place like home.||||reg||1
Z||000010||FieroBUZZ||12-18-2001||07:21 PM||||A guy who worked for our computer company had to do a thorough background check to do work for the Canadian military. Well, his mom and dad were doctors who fled Cuba to the States, and he came to Canada to avoid the draft.  Then he spent 10 years bumming around from commune to commune on a motorcycle. Usually so strung out he didn't know where he was.   So what did he do...made it all up. Evidently they don't do follow ups in Canada.<BR>Gary||||reg||3
Z||000011||flyntus||12-18-2001||07:48 PM||||When I was in the Army, I had a Top Secret/Special Background Investigation (TS/SBI) security clearance.  The FBI talked to the neighbors in my neighborhood, schools I had attended and people who knew me at the various jobs I had.  They had copies of my Arizona, California & North Carolina drivers license records.  They requested copies of my Military Police records from each military post that I had been stationed.  Only 1 turned up anything negative  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> During the last 3 years of my military service, they "found" a California warrant for my arrest for failure to pay a speeding ticket, lucky for me, I had paid by check & I got that squared away.  Believe it or not, they do try to check but it's the age old story too many people to check & not enough resources to do the job.  I've had people wait over a year before I could put them to work because they couldn't get the security clearances. ||||reg||1
